import{_ as e,o as a,c as s,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.k9nOhppk.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"What the benchmarks say, 2026-10-12","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"internals/reviews/benchmarks-review-2026-10-12.md","filePath":"internals/reviews/benchmarks-review-2026-10-12.md","lastUpdated":1791864160000}'),r={name:"internals/reviews/benchmarks-review-2026-10-12.md"};function n(i,t,d,l,h,c){return a(),s("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[o('<h1 id="what-the-benchmarks-say-2026-10-12" tabindex="-1">What the benchmarks say, 2026-10-12 <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-benchmarks-say-2026-10-12" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the benchmarks say, 2026-10-12&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The owner asked for native code that is faster than Go and uses less memory, and for a real program to measure it with. 7.7 built the suite (<a href="./../benchmarks">Benchmarks against Go</a>) and S.1 wrote Eyelang&#39;s lexer in Eyelang, which is the suite&#39;s ninth benchmark. This review reads the numbers and the experience of writing the lexer, and asks one question of each gap: <strong>is it a cost of how we generate code today, or a cost of the language&#39;s rules?</strong> Only the second kind argues for changing syntax, grammar, the standard library or the design philosophy.</p><p>Section 6 gives a recommendation on each of the native plan&#39;s open decisions. <strong>The owner accepted them on 2026-10-12</strong>, with the cheap fixes (7.14), values laid out by type (7.13), B′ hosting and the lexer&#39;s five language changes (G.20); ROADMAP has the tasks.</p><h2 id="the-short-version" tabindex="-1">The short version <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-short-version" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The short version&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>No language rule stands between Eyelang and Go&#39;s speed.</strong> Every gap the profiles show comes from the C back end and its runtime. Two hand-written C experiments show it: fib with Eyelang&#39;s own <code>Int</code> rule (a tagged small integer, checked for overflow) runs as fast as Go, and binary trees with reference counting, a static <code>Leaf</code> and a simple free list run faster than Go.</li><li><strong>The root cause is one assumption in the back end:</strong> every value is one machine word, and everything that isn&#39;t a small number is a separate heap object, decided while printing C. The low IR is already monomorphic, so it knows every type. Values should be laid out by type: enums as small integers, small records as C structs, <code>Option</code> without a box, lists of records as arrays of structs, slices that share bytes.</li><li><strong>The plan&#39;s order needs one change.</strong> The plan put reference counting (7.5a) first. That still matters, but it can&#39;t reach Go alone: deciding which values are counted depends on the layout, so the layout should come first or together. Three cheap fixes come before both: compiling the runtime with the program (20–25% on fib, measured), a size-class allocator instead of musl&#39;s <code>malloc</code>, and static values for variants without fields.</li><li><strong>The lexer asked for five small language changes</strong>, all in line with the pillars: hex literals, string patterns over <code>Bytes</code>, <code>xs[i] = v</code> on a variable, <code>fs.readBytes</code>, and a variant&#39;s number. It also found one checker bug, since fixed. None is fundamental.</li><li><strong>The design philosophy holds.</strong> Values without aliasing are what <em>permit</em> the fast layout (Go can&#39;t put a shared object in a register; Eyelang can, since nothing shares it). Reference counting is not the bottleneck. <code>Int</code> with arbitrary precision costs about Go&#39;s speed on integer code, not more.</li></ul><h2 id="_1-the-numbers" tabindex="-1">1. The numbers <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-the-numbers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. The numbers&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Recorded on 2026-10-12 (4-core Xeon, Go 1.27.2, zig 0.17.0; fastest of 5 runs, peak RSS).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Benchmark</th><th style="text-align:right;">Native ms</th><th style="text-align:right;">Go ms</th><th style="text-align:right;">Time × Go</th><th style="text-align:right;">Native MB</th><th style="text-align:right;">Go MB</th><th style="text-align:right;">Memory × Go</th><th style="text-align:right;">Interpreter ms</th><th style="text-align:right;">Interpreter MB</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>fib(37)</td><td style="text-align:right;">455</td><td style="text-align:right;">96</td><td style="text-align:right;">4.74×</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.16</td><td style="text-align:right;">2.03</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.08×</td><td style="text-align:right;">7,382</td><td style="text-align:right;">18.4</td></tr><tr><td>mandelbrot, 1600²</td><td style="text-align:right;">278</td><td style="text-align:right;">118</td><td style="text-align:right;">2.34×</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.16</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.97</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.08×</td><td style="text-align:right;">26,318</td><td style="text-align:right;">23.5</td></tr><tr><td>trees, depth 16</td><td style="text-align:right;">1,051</td><td style="text-align:right;">468</td><td style="text-align:right;">2.25×</td><td style="text-align:right;">80.2</td><td style="text-align:right;">13.0</td><td style="text-align:right;">6.17×</td><td style="text-align:right;">7,749</td><td style="text-align:right;">50.3</td></tr><tr><td>records, 30M <code>with</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">1,346</td><td style="text-align:right;">284</td><td style="text-align:right;">4.75×</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.16</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.97</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.08×</td><td style="text-align:right;">9,761</td><td style="text-align:right;">21.1</td></tr><tr><td>strings, 1M parts</td><td style="text-align:right;">378</td><td style="text-align:right;">188</td><td style="text-align:right;">2.01×</td><td style="text-align:right;">83.0</td><td style="text-align:right;">43.4</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.91×</td><td style="text-align:right;">3,323</td><td style="text-align:right;">733.7</td></tr><tr><td>lists, 1M</td><td style="text-align:right;">179</td><td style="text-align:right;">162</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.10×</td><td style="text-align:right;">42.3</td><td style="text-align:right;">46.5</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.91×</td><td style="text-align:right;">3,312</td><td style="text-align:right;">131.7</td></tr><tr><td>maps, 5M lookups</td><td style="text-align:right;">548</td><td style="text-align:right;">97</td><td style="text-align:right;">5.67×</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.18</td><td style="text-align:right;">2.53</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.47×</td><td style="text-align:right;">16,824</td><td style="text-align:right;">31.9</td></tr><tr><td>logs, 200k lines</td><td style="text-align:right;">383</td><td style="text-align:right;">263</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.46×</td><td style="text-align:right;">54.7</td><td style="text-align:right;">59.9</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.91×</td><td style="text-align:right;">1,671</td><td style="text-align:right;">81.8</td></tr><tr><td>lexer, 7.5 MB of Eyelang</td><td style="text-align:right;">1,786</td><td style="text-align:right;">388</td><td style="text-align:right;">4.61×</td><td style="text-align:right;">478.0</td><td style="text-align:right;">191.8</td><td style="text-align:right;">2.49×</td><td style="text-align:right;">28,247</td><td style="text-align:right;">1,798.8</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>After 7.14</strong> (section 3&#39;s cheap fixes), measured before and after on another host, time × Go: trees 2.94× → 1.13×, strings 1.65× → 0.63×, logs 1.49× → 0.94×, maps 5.70× → 3.89×, records 9.39× → 7.08×, fib 4.23× → 3.62×, the lexer 3.29× → 2.98× (its native time halved, 2,918 → 1,540 ms, and its memory fell from 477 to 200 MB); 1.90× Go&#39;s time on the geometric mean. The table above is the review&#39;s evidence and stays as it was; the native plan&#39;s <a href="./native-plan-2026-10-12#against-go">Against Go</a> has the rest.</p><p><strong>After 7.5 and 7.13</strong> (counting in the low IR, and values laid out by type), recorded in <a href="./../benchmarks">Benchmarks against Go</a>, time × Go: records 7.08× → 0.53×, the lexer 2.98× → 0.65× (its memory 200 → 63 MB, 0.33× Go&#39;s), trees 1.13× → 0.66× (25 → 8.4 MB), fib 3.62× → 1.69×, maps 3.89× → 1.62×, lists 1.38× → 1.00× (42 → 20 MB); 0.85× Go&#39;s time and 0.36× its memory on the geometric mean. That bears out section 1&#39;s diagnosis: the layout, with precise counting, was the larger cost, and no language rule stood in the way. Maps, fib and mandelbrot are what remain.</p><p>Native code takes 1.1–5.7× Go&#39;s time, 2.8× on the geometric mean, and uses less memory in six of nine (0.57× on the geometric mean). Four of those six win only on start-up: a static binary starts at 0.16 MB and a Go program at 2 MB. That floor is real and useful, since a program that mostly waits is mostly floor, but it isn&#39;t evidence about how we hold data. Trees, strings and the lexer hold data and use more memory than Go.</p><p>The interpreter takes 4–95× native code&#39;s time. Its memory starts at about 18 MB, the Go runtime and the checked program, and grows to 734 MB on strings and 1.8 GB on the lexer, 3.8× native code&#39;s and 9.4× Go&#39;s there; on trees it holds less than native code does today (50 MB against 80), since Go&#39;s collector frees each tree soon after it is checked. After R.6 it is in CPython&#39;s range, and the lab, which is bound by input and output, runs its 31 tests in under 200 ms. The interpreter is not what limits the lab today.</p><h2 id="_2-two-experiments-what-the-rules-cost" tabindex="-1">2. Two experiments: what the rules cost <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-two-experiments-what-the-rules-cost" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Two experiments: what the rules cost&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The profiles (callgrind on the <code>-O2</code> binaries, in the native plan&#39;s <a href="./native-plan-2026-10-12#against-go">Against Go</a>) say where the time goes. They don&#39;t say how far a better back end could get. Two small C programs, compiled with the pinned zig at <code>-O2</code> for static musl as <code>eye build</code> does, answer that for the two rules most likely to cost something.</p><p><strong><code>Int</code> has arbitrary precision.</strong> fib(37), written three ways:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th style="text-align:right;">ms</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plain <code>int64</code>, as Go does it</td><td style="text-align:right;">54–66</td></tr><tr><td><code>int64</code> with an overflow check on <code>+</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">90–115</td></tr><tr><td>Eyelang&#39;s rule: a tagged word, small values inline, an overflow branch to a slow path</td><td style="text-align:right;">93–118</td></tr><tr><td>Go</td><td style="text-align:right;">96</td></tr><tr><td><code>eye build</code> today</td><td style="text-align:right;">455</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The rule costs about 1.6–1.9× against plain C, because the overflow check stops the C compiler&#39;s rearranging of the recursion, and lands at Go&#39;s speed. The tag itself costs almost nothing beyond the check. So almost all of today&#39;s 4.7× is the back end: a <code>dup</code>/<code>drop</code> on every move, <code>&lt;</code> as a call into another file, every local dropped at return. Compiling the emitted C as one unit with link-time optimisation alone takes fib from about 600 to about 450 ms on the same machine.</p><p><strong>Reference counting instead of a tracing collector.</strong> The trees benchmark in C with reference counts: 24-byte nodes (count and two children), <code>Leaf</code> a static value, each tree freed as soon as it is checked:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th style="text-align:right;">ms</th><th style="text-align:right;">MB</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>musl&#39;s <code>malloc</code> and <code>free</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">461–573</td><td style="text-align:right;">16</td></tr><tr><td>A free list for the node size</td><td style="text-align:right;">313–425</td><td style="text-align:right;">16</td></tr><tr><td>Go</td><td style="text-align:right;">468</td><td style="text-align:right;">13</td></tr><tr><td><code>eye build</code> today</td><td style="text-align:right;">1,051</td><td style="text-align:right;">80</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Reference counting with a size-class allocator beats Go&#39;s collector on its best benchmark. What costs today is a fresh object for every <code>Leaf</code>, 40-byte nodes, trees that live until their variable is reassigned or the function returns, and musl&#39;s slow <code>malloc</code>.</p><h2 id="_3-back-end-costs-and-what-removes-each" tabindex="-1">3. Back-end costs, and what removes each <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-back-end-costs-and-what-removes-each" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Back-end costs, and what removes each&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These are costs of the current code generation, not of the language. Each line says what removes it, roughly by payoff.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Cost</th><th>Where it shows</th><th>Remedy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Every value is one word; records, variants and <code>Option</code> are heap objects</td><td>records 4.8×, maps 5.7×, lexer 4.6× and 2.5× memory, trees</td><td><strong>Values laid out by type</strong> (new task, section 5): field-less enums as integers, small records as C structs passed by value, <code>Option(T)</code> without a box, <code>List(Record)</code> as an array of structs, <code>Bytes</code> and <code>String</code> slices that share storage</td></tr><tr><td>A <code>dup</code>/<code>drop</code> on every move, every local dropped at return</td><td>fib 60% of instructions, trees&#39; 80 MB</td><td>7.5a: counting in the low IR, drop after last use, borrowed parameters</td></tr><tr><td><code>{ p with … }</code> copies because <code>p</code> is still live</td><td>records</td><td>7.5a&#39;s &quot;in place when unique and dead&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>The runtime is compiled file by file, so nothing inlines (<code>ey_int_cmp</code>, <code>divmod</code>)</td><td>fib, mandelbrot, maps</td><td>One translation unit, or LTO: 20–25% on fib, measured</td></tr><tr><td>musl&#39;s <code>malloc</code></td><td>trees, lexer (allocation is about a third of the lexer&#39;s instructions)</td><td>A size-class allocator in the runtime: 30% on trees, measured</td></tr><tr><td>A field-less variant allocates each time</td><td>trees, lexer (each token&#39;s kind)</td><td>A static value per variant (until enums become integers)</td></tr><tr><td><code>&quot;{n}&quot;</code> formats through <code>snprintf</code>; <code>split</code> copies parts</td><td>strings</td><td>Integer formatting in the runtime; substrings that share bytes</td></tr><tr><td><code>sort</code>, hashing and equality go through type descriptors</td><td>lists 42%, maps</td><td>Specialise them per type, as the low IR already specialises functions</td></tr><tr><td><code>xs += [x]</code> builds a one-element list</td><td>lexer</td><td>Lower it to an append</td></tr><tr><td>Passing a named function builds a closure</td><td>lexer</td><td>A static closure for a function without captures</td></tr><tr><td><code>/</code> and <code>%</code> by a constant call <code>divmod</code></td><td>lists, maps, the lexer&#39;s checksum</td><td>Constant divisors inline (the C compiler does the rest)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>None of these needs a language change. Most are small; the layout is the large one.</p><h2 id="_4-language-costs-and-what-the-lexer-asked-for" tabindex="-1">4. Language costs, and what the lexer asked for <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-language-costs-and-what-the-lexer-asked-for" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Language costs, and what the lexer asked for&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="rules-that-cost-something" tabindex="-1">Rules that cost something <a class="header-anchor" href="#rules-that-cost-something" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rules that cost something&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong><code>Int</code> with arbitrary precision.</strong> About Go&#39;s speed on integer code (section 2), so it doesn&#39;t stop the bar on most programs. A tight integer loop can still lose to Go by the overflow check. If that matters later, the low IR can prove some values small (a loop counter bounded by a list&#39;s size) and drop the check. The fixed-width types (G.1) already exist for code that wants wrapping. <strong>No change recommended.</strong></li><li><strong>Indexing returns <code>Option</code>.</strong> Free once <code>Option</code> has no box: a branch, like Go&#39;s bounds check. <strong>No change.</strong></li><li><strong>No element assignment.</strong> <code>xs[i] = v</code> is an error, so code that changes one element rebuilds the list, which is linear where Go is constant. This one is a rule, and it costs real algorithms. See below.</li></ul><h3 id="what-writing-the-lexer-asked-for" tabindex="-1">What writing the lexer asked for <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-writing-the-lexer-asked-for" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What writing the lexer asked for&quot;">​</a></h3><p>S.1&#39;s lexer is about 400 lines of Eyelang against 557 of Go, gives the same 24,959 tokens on the conformance suite and corpus, and reads well: byte patterns such as <code>[&#39;.&#39;, &#39;.&#39;, &#39;=&#39;, ..] =&gt; Some((DotDotEq, 3))</code> say what Go&#39;s switch statements spell out. What got in the way is open question 43. Recommendations:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Ask</th><th>Kind</th><th>Recommendation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>xs[i] = v</code> on a <code>var</code> or <code>mut</code> list (and <code>m[k] = v</code> on a map)</td><td>Rule, small syntax</td><td><strong>Yes.</strong> It is local, visible mutation, as <code>p.x = v</code> already is for records, and with uniqueness it is in place. Removes the linear rebuild</td></tr><tr><td>Hex (and binary) literals: <code>0xC2..=0xDF</code></td><td>Grammar</td><td><strong>Yes.</strong> The notation is universal; byte-level code is unreadable in decimal</td></tr><tr><td>A string literal as a pattern over <code>Bytes</code>: <code>&quot;continue&quot;</code> instead of eight characters</td><td>Typing rule, no new syntax</td><td><strong>Yes.</strong> The literal already exists; a pattern over <code>Bytes</code> accepts it</td></tr><tr><td><code>fs.readBytes</code>, and <code>Bytes</code> from a <code>List(Byte)</code></td><td>Library</td><td><strong>Yes.</strong> Bytes that aren&#39;t text must be readable and testable</td></tr><tr><td>A variant&#39;s number (Go&#39;s <code>int(kind)</code>)</td><td>Library (a derived method)</td><td><strong>Yes, as a derived method on enums without fields</strong>, not syntax. Name to choose</td></tr><tr><td>A variant named like a built-in type (<code>Int</code>) takes the type&#39;s name over in its module</td><td>Checker bug</td><td><strong>Fixed</strong> in 104fa0d: a variant isn&#39;t a type</td></tr><tr><td><code>if let</code>, escaping <code>{</code> in test sources</td><td>Ergonomics</td><td>No change now; <code>match</code> covers <code>if let</code>, and raw strings are open question territory</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="the-design-philosophy-checked" tabindex="-1">The design philosophy, checked <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-design-philosophy-checked" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The design philosophy, checked&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Pillar 1, values without aliasing.</strong> The benchmarks strengthen it. Because no value is shared, the compiler may copy a small record into registers, store a list of records inline and share a string&#39;s bytes between slices, without anything able to notice. These are the layout changes section 3 needs. Go can&#39;t do the first for a pointer it has handed out.</li><li><strong>Performance by construction: reference counting, in place, no collector.</strong> Holds, with an allocator to match (section 2). The cost model a reader sees doesn&#39;t change.</li><li><strong>A static binary on musl.</strong> Holds, but musl&#39;s <code>malloc</code> and <code>printf</code> are slow, so the runtime brings its own allocator and number formatting rather than relying on the C library.</li><li><strong>Go as the bar.</strong> A fair bar for time. For memory it is fair on data the program holds; the start-up floor favours Eyelang, and the bar for a program with entities or jobs under B′ includes the Go runner, as the plan says.</li></ul><h2 id="_5-assumptions-that-turned-out-wrong" tabindex="-1">5. Assumptions that turned out wrong <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-assumptions-that-turned-out-wrong" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Assumptions that turned out wrong&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>&quot;Perceus will make it fast.&quot;</strong> compilation.md and the plan treat precise reference counting (7.5) as the main performance step. It is necessary, but the larger cost is the uniform one-word representation. With it, even perfect counting leaves records, <code>Option</code>, enums and lists of records on the heap.</li><li><strong>&quot;Counting first, layout later.&quot;</strong> The low IR decides which values are counted from their types. Adding <code>Dup</code>/<code>Drop</code> before the layout is decided means doing it twice.</li><li><strong>&quot;musl is fine.&quot;</strong> It is fine for portability and a static file, not for allocation-heavy code.</li><li><strong>&quot;Each runtime file compiled once and cached.&quot;</strong> It makes rebuilds fast and stops inlining across files. LTO or one unit costs build time once per program and gains 20–25%.</li><li><strong>&quot;The interpreter is the bottleneck.&quot;</strong> After R.6 it isn&#39;t, for the lab. Native code matters for computation-heavy programs and for the self-hosted compiler, and its start-up floor (0.16 MB, 0.3 ms) matters for many small waiting programs.</li></ol><p><strong>Proposed new task: 7.13, values laid out by type.</strong> The C back end and the low IR choose each type&#39;s representation: field-less enums as integers, small records as structs by value, <code>Option(T)</code> without a box, lists of fixed-size values as arrays, string and byte slices sharing storage. Done when records, maps and the lexer are within Go&#39;s time and the lexer&#39;s memory is below Go&#39;s, with every cross-check passing.</p><h2 id="_6-the-native-plan-s-decisions-in-the-light-of-the-numbers" tabindex="-1">6. The native plan&#39;s decisions, in the light of the numbers <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-the-native-plan-s-decisions-in-the-light-of-the-numbers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. The native plan&#39;s decisions, in the light of the numbers&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Decision</th><th>Recommendation now</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Split 7.5 into 7.5a (counting in the low IR) and 7.5b (7.12: reuse, fault regions)</td><td><strong>Yes, and put 7.13 (layout) before or with 7.5a.</strong> Before both, one small task for the cheap fixes: one translation unit or LTO, a size-class allocator, static field-less variants, integer formatting, constant division</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Hosting for 7.6</td><td><strong>B′.</strong> The benchmarks don&#39;t touch it. A crossing under B′ costs about 35 ns; the lexer spends about 780 ns of compiled code per token today, and a job&#39;s crossings are its entity calls and steps, which wait on input and output anyway. 7.10&#39;s count stays useful for the record, not for the choice</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>The store and tasks stay in the Go runner</td><td><strong>Yes.</strong> Nothing in the numbers asks otherwise</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>v0.4&#39;s exit test</td><td><strong>Keep &quot;the digest runs as a native job&quot;,</strong> and record the interpreted digest&#39;s idle memory and resume time now (R.7 gives them) as the numbers to beat</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Order</td><td><strong>7.8 and the cheap fixes now</strong> (no decision needed beyond this), <strong>then 7.13 with 7.5a, then 7.9, 7.10, 7.6; 7.11 tracks the bar throughout; 7.12 last.</strong> Phase 6&#39;s exit test (6.6, 6.8) is independent and waits on the homelab</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>A <code>wasm32-wasi</code> target</td><td><strong>Later.</strong> Unchanged by the numbers</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>The bar: per benchmark or on the mean</td><td><strong>Per benchmark, for time and for memory on data held.</strong> Count the start-up floor separately, so a win on it isn&#39;t read as a win on layout</td></tr></tbody></table><p>And for open question 43: the five changes in section 4, as one task with a spec change first, independent of the native work. The lexer is their test: it gets shorter and reads better.</p><h2 id="where-the-evidence-is" tabindex="-1">Where the evidence is <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-the-evidence-is" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where the evidence is&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The suite: <code>testdata/bench</code>, <code>go run ./tools/bench</code> (<code>--write</code>, <code>--check</code>), results in <a href="./../benchmarks">Benchmarks against Go</a> and <code>testdata/bench/results.json</code>.</li><li>The lexer: <code>testdata/programs/lexer</code>, checked by <code>interp/lexer_test.go</code> and natively by <code>cgen</code>&#39;s <code>TestLexerBinary</code>.</li><li>The profiles: the native plan&#39;s <a href="./native-plan-2026-10-12#against-go">Against Go</a>.</li><li>The two C experiments of section 2 are small enough to repeat: fib and trees written by hand, built with <code>go run ./tools/zig cc -target x86_64-linux-musl -O2 -fno-sanitize=undefined</code>, and the emitted C of <code>testdata/bench/fib</code> built with and without <code>-flto</code>.</li></ul>',40)])])}const m=e(r,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
